Title: "Type Resolution"
Id: "2b59cf98-6cf3-4b4b-835d-baabd8ee216b"

Text: "The spelling of a binary operator selects its resolution path.

For a plain symbolic operator such as `x * y`:

1. A bound `*` is applied as `*(x, y)`.
2. Otherwise, a `Disambiguates:` entry selects the first matching `when:`
   branch in source order, or its `else:`. If the entry exists but no branch
   matches and no fallback is present, resolution fails.
3. Only when no `Disambiguates:` entry exists does the checker try a capability
   whose owner type both operands satisfy. This path first materializes the
   available specification and membership reductions as type facts.

Colon-qualified operators go directly to capability lookup:

```mlg-fragment
x :* y
x *: y
x :*: y
```

The first selects an owner type satisfied by `x`, the second by `y`, and the
third by both operands. Extension chains can supply that owner type. The
checker takes the first matching registered capability; it does not calculate
or rank a least common ancestor. These paths do not fall back to
`Disambiguates:` and do not first materialize specification or membership
reductions. The chosen capability's target is then checked with the actual
operands substituted, so its requirements still apply to both operands.

Plain named operators are function-call sugar: `x |op| y` means `op(x, y)`.
Colon-qualified named operators use the same ownership modes as symbolic ones.
Plain `=` and `!=` try a common-owner capability and otherwise remain builtin
statements even when the operand types have no equality capability.

Capabilities from `Requires:` and `Enables:` are considered together, and
lookup follows `Declares:` extension chains. `from:` capabilities apply to a
compatible built collection literal. Assignment-backed `specifies:` facts are
normalized so capabilities stated in terms of a component can follow that
component's assigned command value. Views do not select ordinary operator
capabilities or disambiguation branches."
Id: "b4f93e01-3b86-4228-b3ed-512add4b38bf"

SectionTitle: "Builds And Views"
Id: "88aed822-a5b4-4f56-8966-ee206c1c5290"

Text: "A build `\type@value` succeeds when the value already has the type, reaches it
through extension, or has an applicable `Enables: view:`.

```mlg-fragment
Enables:
. view:
  as: r := \as.rational{n} is \rational
  signifies: n \.embedded.to./ r
```

The view symbol, its value, and its type are available to `signifies:`. If the
view expression is a name or tuple of names, facts follow through those concrete
components; otherwise only the advertised type is known. Views satisfy resolved
command requirements and builds. A view can also supply the operand type needed
to resolve an infix specification operator. It does not select ordinary
overloaded operators or `Disambiguates:` branches.

Hard builds (`@!`), hard declarations (`is!`), `\\coercion`, `\\encoding`, and
the old `Enables: relation:` form are not supported."
Id: "40a048de-3573-4d73-ad8c-ca8c1772687d"
